			<item>
				<title>Lessons Learned from Pixar</title>
				<link>https://justaddwater.dk/2010/04/28/lessons-learned-from-pixar-2/</link>
				<pubDate>Wed, 28 Apr 2010 09:46:29 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>https://justaddwater.dk/2010/04/28/lessons-learned-from-pixar-2/</guid>
				<description>&lt;p&gt;I took Jeff Atwodd&amp;rsquo;s advice and watched Ed Catmull of Pixar: &amp;ldquo;Keep your crises small&amp;rdquo;. A presentation about lessons learned from successes and crises at Pixar.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;A central quote:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;If you give a good idea to a mediocre group, they&amp;rsquo;ll screw it up. &lt;strong&gt;If you give a mediocre idea to a good group, they&amp;rsquo;ll fix it.&lt;/strong&gt; Or they&amp;rsquo;ll throw it away and come up with something else.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;We think of &amp;lsquo;an idea&amp;rsquo; – for movies, for products. It&amp;rsquo;s usually thought of as some singular thing. But the reality of these successful movies – as with all &lt;strong&gt;successful products – that they have got thousands of ideas&lt;/strong&gt;. It&amp;rsquo;s all sorts of things necessary to make the film successful. And you have to &lt;strong&gt;get most of them right to be successful&lt;/strong&gt;. That is why you need a team that works well together.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>Don&#39;t Repeat Yourself (Unless Reading Book)</title>
				<link>https://justaddwater.dk/2008/06/23/dont-repeat-yourself-unless-reading-book/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 23 Jun 2008 12:13:38 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>https://justaddwater.dk/2008/06/23/dont-repeat-yourself-unless-reading-book/</guid>
				<description>&lt;div style=&#34;float: left; margin: 0 10px 10px 0&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;http://www.pragprog.com/titles/tpp/the-pragmatic-programmer&#34;&gt;&lt;img src=&#34;http://www.pragprog.com/images/covers/190x228/tpp.jpg?1184184147&#34; alt=&#34;The Pragmatic Programmer&#34; title=&#34;pragmatic programmer book image&#34; height=&#34;228&#34; width=&#34;190&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;After reading Pragmatic Programmer at my recent vacation, I immediately found an urge to repeat myself and read it again.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Ironic that one of the most popular tips from it is &amp;ldquo;Don&amp;rsquo;t repeat yourself&amp;rdquo;. Even though the book was published in 1999 (before the &lt;a href=&#34;http://agilemanifesto.org/&#34;&gt;agile manifesto&lt;/a&gt;), there are so many golden nuggets in the book, that I find myself constantly using it at work.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>The Dark Side of Information Architecture</title>
